{
 "id": "r6-tanwin",
 "title": "Tanwīn and tāʾ marbūṭa",
 "ar": "التَّنْوِين",
 "goal": "Read the -un / -an / -in endings and the feminine ending ة.",
 "noAutoVocab": true,
 "words": [],
 "understand": [
  {
   "h": "Tanwīn: a vowel + n"
  },
  {
   "p": "A doubled vowel mark at the end of a noun adds an <strong>n</strong> sound. This is tanwīn. For now, think of it as the “a / an” form: <span class='ar'>كِتَابٌ</span> kitābun = a book."
  },
  {
   "table": {
    "headers": [
     "Mark",
     "Sound",
     "Example"
    ],
    "ar": [
     2
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "two ḍammas",
      "-un",
      "كِتَابٌ"
     ],
     [
      "two fatḥas (+ an extra ا)",
      "-an",
      "كِتَابًا"
     ],
     [
      "two kasras",
      "-in",
      "كِتَابٍ"
     ]
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "rule": "With two fatḥas, an extra <span class='ar'>ا</span> is written at the end but not pronounced as long: <span class='ar'>كِتَابًا</span> is read kitāban. (Words ending in ة don't take this extra alif.)"
  },
  {
   "h": "Tāʾ marbūṭa ة"
  },
  {
   "p": "The letter <span class='ar'>ة</span> (a hāʾ with two dots) appears only at the end of words and usually marks them as <strong>feminine</strong>. Say it as <em>-t</em> when a vowel follows, and as a soft <em>-ah</em> when you stop on it."
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "مَدْرَسَةٌ",
     "en": "madrasatun (madrasah when you stop)"
    },
    {
     "ar": "سَيَّارَةٌ",
     "en": "sayyāratun: car"
    },
    {
     "ar": "غُرْفَةٌ",
     "en": "ghurfatun: room"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tip": "When you stop at the end of a sentence, drop the final short vowel and tanwīn: <span class='ar'>كِتَابٌ</span> → kitāb. With -an, stop on a long ā: <span class='ar'>كِتَابًا</span> → kitābā."
  }
 ],
